            <ArticleTitle>[Infectious risk related to the formation of multi-species biofilms (Candida - bacteria) on peripheral vascular catheters].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>20-27</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1156-5233(16)30172-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.mycmed.2016.10.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The Candida yeasts are the fourth leading cause of death from systemic infections, the risk may increase when the infection also involves bacteria. Yeasts and bacteria can adhere to medical implants, such as peripheral vascular catheters, and form a multicellular structures called &quot;mixed biofilms&quot; more resistant to antimicrobials agents. However, the formation of mixed biofilms on implants leads to long-term persistent infections because they can act as reservoirs of pathogens that have poorly understood interactions.</AbstractText>
